SPI Registers
-------------

	SPI0 

		* S0SPCR
		* S0SPCCR
		* S0SPDR
		* S0SPSR
	

Is it possible for SPI protocol to communicate with 2 masters or controllers ?

	microcontroller to microcontroller not possible using SPI protocol 

	The solution is to make one microcontroller as master and the other microcontroller as slave 



SPI Control Register (S0SPCR)
-----------------------------
            
	   7 	  6 	 5   	4	   3	    2	       1	     0
	[ SPIE | LSBF | MSTR | CPOL | CPHA | Reserved | Reserved | Reserved ]

	MSTR :- Master mode select 
	LSBF :- LSB first control 	
	SPIE :- Serial peripheral interrupt enable 

	MSTR : 0 => (SPI Operates in slave mode)
	MSTR : 1 => (SPI operates in master mode)

	LSBF :- 1 => SPI data is transferred LSB first
	LSBF :- 0 => SPI data is transferred MSB first 


	Slave comes with predefined features, we need to set same settings with the master 


SPI Clock Counter Register (S0SPCCR)
-----------------------------------

	This register controls the frequency of a masters SCK (clock pin)

	    31		         8 7		                   0 	
        [   Reserved     ] [ SPIO clock counter setting]

		
	SPI speed = pclk / S0SPCRR

	S0SPCCR = 15; 	// SPIO freq is 1 Mbps 
	S0SPCCR = 150; 	// SPIO freq is 100 kbps 


SPI Data Register (S0SPDR)
--------------------------

	* This is shift register of SPI0 (SPI Master)
	* Size is 1 byte and it is bi-directional
	* Master transmitted data must be written to this reg, then only SPI transfer will start
	* Master received data (slave transmitted data) can be read from this reg when the flag is set


SPI Status Register (S0SPSR)
----------------------------

	   7 	  6 	 5 	     4     3	    2	       1 	      0 
	[ SPIF | WCOL | ROVR | MODF | ABRT | Reserved | Reserved | Reserved ]

	SPIF :- SPI transfer complete flag
	WCOL :- Write collision 
	ROVR :- Read Overrun 
	MODF :- Mode fault
	ABRT :- Slave Abort 

	SPIF :- SPI data transfer complete flag, when 1 this indicates spi data transfer is complete
	
	Before Finishing the first 1 byte transfer, if the new data is assigned to S0SPDR Reg then write collision occurs 

						  _	
	Before completing the SPI transfer it the CS pin becomes 1 then ABRT bit will set and is called as slave abort.

	If the data of the first transfer is overwritten by the second data transfer then ROVR will set

	Mode fault : Occurs when the case of multi master communication (both are master)








